WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.050
Although it looks like an ant,

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:04.100
This is actually a spider.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.400
Myrmarachne formicaria is a jumping spider

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.380
which has evolved its ant-like appearance

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:11.080
as a clever ruse to evade predators.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.320
Similar evolutionary adaptations have been
found in the past,

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.580
such as butterflies that look like their less tasty cousins

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.780
and beetles that look like scary wasps.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.220
Researchers looked at these tiny arachnids
with a high speed camera, and they found that

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.820
the spiders not only look like ants, but they
walk like ants too.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.280
When marching along, the spider walks in a
winding wave motion,

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.780
which appears to potential predators as an ant following a trail.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.480
It was previously thought that these ant-like
spiders don’t use their front legs at all

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.940
when walking. Instead, raising them up as pantomime antennae.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.840
This new research shows that when viewed in slow motion, the spiders move

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.600
using all 8 legs, only raising their front
ones whenever they take a pause.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.030
These pauses are another piece of mimicry.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:03.960
The stops are consistently spaced out and
timed long enough to nail the illusion.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.340
In most species of jumping spiders, raising
the forelimbs is a sign of aggression,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.200
so in the future, researchers would like to see
if other spiders are fooled.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.300
Do they see a threatening spider or just another
ant?

